# A generator of a height-one prime of K[X] has order 1

Theorem statement: `AlgebraicCurve.RationalFunctionField.ord_ofHeightOneSpectrum_of_span`
(`89bb3391-aada-5f34-8ae4-964805dcc2c2`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `8f9aa023-829c-5b7d-a779-3de65f788e61`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $K$ be a field, and let $w$ be a point of the height-one spectrum of the polynomial ring $K[X]$,
i.e. a nonzero prime ideal `w.asIdeal` of height one. Let $p \in K[X]$ be nonzero and assume that
`w.asIdeal` is the principal ideal generated by $p$. Associated with $w$ is the place
`Place.ofHeightOneSpectrum w` of the rational function field $\mathrm{RatFunc}(K)$ over $K$: the
valuation subring of the $w$-adic valuation of $\mathrm{RatFunc}(K)$, packaged as a `Place`, that
is, a valuation subring of $\mathrm{RatFunc}(K)$ containing the image of $K$, different from the
whole field, and a principal ideal ring. For a place $v$, $v.\mathrm{ord}(f)$ is defined as minus
the `WithZero.log` of the value at $f$ of the adic valuation attached to $v$, valued in
$\mathbb{Z}^{m0}$, so that $\mathrm{ord}$ is the additive normalisation of the valuation with
$\mathrm{ord}$ of a uniformiser equal to $1$. The assertion is that the order of the image of $p$
under the structure map $K[X] \to \mathrm{RatFunc}(K)$ at this place equals $1$; equivalently, any
generator of $w$ is a uniformiser at the corresponding finite place of $K(t)/K$.

This is the basic normalisation statement for the finite places of the rational function field: the
finite places of $K(t)$ correspond to the height-one primes of $K[X]$, and a generator of such a
prime has order exactly one there. It is used throughout the treatment of divisors on
$\mathbb{P}^1$, for instance in computing degrees of principal divisors and in evaluating functions
at places, including at the place at infinity.

